A||N||9||mpicanso||87GT radio question||||3
Z||000000||mpicanso||09-16-1999||08:05 AM||||Well I got back my car yesterday from PEP Boys (they are the one's who installed the new heater core not a bad price only 150 bucks).  Anyways thanks to everyone who helped me find out what the problem was. My next project is to install a new radio in the car I would like to have a CD player any ideas which brand I should use?  I have the factory sub-woofer and would like to keep it working.||||reg||3
Z||000001||Dave85||09-16-1999||08:33 AM||||The Kenwoods are really nice. I would look around at Circuit City or Best Buys before going to a stereo store.  Circuit City carries most of the top names(Alpine,Kenwood). I almost bought a Kenwood but decided to shell out the $$ on a Dennon. BTW installing one is very easy. DON'T use the the trim kit from Wally World, IT SUCKS!  The one from C.C. is pretty good. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||K9||09-16-1999||08:39 AM||||Well if you get a after market CD player, Try to get a "GM" size. Many Brands now make it but you will have to look around to find it. If you go with the regular size, you will need to use a addapter face to fill the space and it dosent look too good. Good luck||||reg||14
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-16-1999||09:53 AM||||I have a bottom-of-the-line Blaupunkt I bought in 6/94 that I installed in my 84 Coupe, then removed and installed in my 88 Coupe.  It is still going strong.  I would definitely buy another.<P>I used the Crutchfield installation kit.  The kit fills the extra space above and below the radio nicely, but the radio does protrude about 1/2" forward from the trim plate.  If this would bother you, I'd consider checking junkyards for a GM/Delco factory CD player. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mpicanso||09-16-1999||09:59 AM||||Thanks for all the info.  After I install an after-market CD player will the factory sub-woofer still work?||||reg||10
Z||000005||TOM||09-16-1999||11:02 AM||||Yes mine works with my clarion. I made my own wiring adapter.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||09-16-1999||09:40 PM||||I have a Sony CD player in my 87 SE, a Pioneer large GM style CD player in the Batmobile, and a Kenwood in my 85 GT. I like all three, but I think the Pioneer looks much better and it doesn't need a mounting kit.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WGraffiKs||09-21-1999||05:48 PM||||I was about to post a question on this exact subject, but I'll put it here first!  My new JVC CD stereo won't work with the stock sub-woofer amp.  The amp, which is wired in parallel to the high-output side (speakers) of the OEM stereo, "motorboats" (for the electronics techies out there), then feeds back through the stereo to shut it down (audio only).  The stock speakers are 11 ohms.  The amp inputs are from all 4 speakers.  Does anyone know how to make the stock sub-woofer work with this foreign front-end?  BTW, I believe the Fiero (Delco) OEM radio is known as a DIN 1/2.  If your setup is that size, it should fit the opening just like stock.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TooLowFiero||09-29-1999||12:54 AM||||i have a pioneer deh p8000 model cd deck in my fiero i have that in my 86 2m4 and a pioneer deh p1000 in my 84 se the 1000 model has skipped once after having it 4 months  and it only cost me 159 bucks the 8000 model was pricey ($650) but with 10 subwoofers in my fiero i needed all the options and if ya all are wondering how i put 10 subwoofers in my car  8 8" subwoofers behind the seat and 2 10" in the front compartment the system cost me 3000 to be put in  but i now have 2 awards cuz of it  it is worth it<P>TooLow||||reg||10
Z||000009||WGraffiKs||09-29-1999||01:55 AM||||10 sub-woofers?  Guess THAT's an easy way to get rid of that nasty throaty noise that happens each time after the ignition key is turned!!!||||reg||12
